1
00:00:05,119 --> 00:00:07,335
A monk sits in deep meditation.

2
00:00:07,415 --> 00:00:10,759
His mind becomes still,
bright, and steady.

3
00:00:10,839 --> 00:00:16,158
And then, the old texts say, it can
be turned toward remarkable things.

4
00:00:16,996 --> 00:00:20,544
He knows what is in the mind of
the person sitting across from him.

5
00:00:20,624 --> 00:00:24,402
He hears sounds from far
away, human and heavenly.

6
00:00:24,482 --> 00:00:26,671
He remembers lives before this one.

7
00:00:26,751 --> 00:00:32,106
He sees beings dying and being reborn,
each according to what they have done.

8
00:00:32,186 --> 00:00:35,358
He passes through a wall
as if it were open air.

9
00:00:36,173 --> 00:00:39,258
Buddhist tradition calls
these the higher knowledges.

10
00:00:39,338 --> 00:00:42,619
In Sanskrit, abhijñā. There are six.

11
00:00:42,735 --> 00:00:45,916
Five are powers that come
with deep concentration.

12
00:00:45,996 --> 00:00:47,587
The sixth is different.

13
00:00:47,667 --> 00:00:52,614
It is the ending of the defilements, the
habits of craving and confusion that bind

14
00:00:52,694 --> 00:00:55,431
a mind. It is awakening itself.

15
00:00:56,241 --> 00:01:00,223
Modern listeners tend to meet
these claims in one of two ways.

16
00:01:00,303 --> 00:01:02,125
Some dismiss them at once.

17
00:01:02,205 --> 00:01:05,998
Matter does not behave like
that, so they must be impossible.

18
00:01:06,078 --> 00:01:08,022
Others embrace them as proof.

19
00:01:08,102 --> 00:01:12,968
Proof of a soul that travels, or a
mind that stands outside the body,

20
00:01:13,048 --> 00:01:17,959
or a higher reality that the
meditator finally sees as it truly is.

21
00:01:18,794 --> 00:01:21,228
These two reactions look like opposites.

22
00:01:21,308 --> 00:01:22,994
But they share one mistake.

23
00:01:23,074 --> 00:01:26,568
Each assumes that something
exists all by itself,

24
00:01:26,648 --> 00:01:30,385
and is the final measure of what
can happen. For one it is matter.

25
00:01:30,465 --> 00:01:32,985
For the other it is mind, or soul.

26
00:01:33,798 --> 00:01:38,012
The Middle Way of Nāgārjuna
refuses that assumption everywhere.

27
00:01:38,092 --> 00:01:40,267
It exempts nothing from the search.

28
00:01:40,347 --> 00:01:45,041
And the higher knowledges turn out to
be a perfect test of what that means.

29
00:01:45,874 --> 00:01:48,743
Start with the word that
the Middle Way takes apart.

30
00:01:48,823 --> 00:01:52,139
In Sanskrit it is svabhāva, own nature.

31
00:01:52,219 --> 00:01:56,501
A thing would have own nature if it were
what it is entirely from its own side.

32
00:01:56,581 --> 00:01:59,639
Not made. Not dependent on anything else.

33
00:01:59,719 --> 00:02:01,600
Not changing in its essence.

34
00:02:02,401 --> 00:02:06,451
Anything taken to have such a
nature can be called a substance.

35
00:02:06,531 --> 00:02:08,408
It need not be a material stuff.

36
00:02:08,488 --> 00:02:12,103
Matter can be taken as the
one thing that is really real.

37
00:02:12,183 --> 00:02:14,372
So can mind, or awareness.

38
00:02:14,452 --> 00:02:18,394
So can a self that stays the
same beneath every experience.

39
00:02:18,474 --> 00:02:22,837
So can a space, an empty box that
would exist with nothing in it.

40
00:02:22,917 --> 00:02:26,034
So can a hidden ground from
which everything comes.

41
00:02:26,850 --> 00:02:29,950
They look very different. But
they all play the same role.

42
00:02:30,030 --> 00:02:33,876
Each is a candidate for the floor
that everything else stands on,

43
00:02:33,956 --> 00:02:35,552
while it stands on nothing.

44
00:02:36,369 --> 00:02:40,460
The Middle Way does not refute substance
by announcing that there is none.

45
00:02:40,540 --> 00:02:41,465
It looks.

46
00:02:41,545 --> 00:02:46,248
If a thing exists by its own nature,
then a careful search should find it,

47
00:02:46,328 --> 00:02:50,464
standing apart from its parts,
its causes, and its name.

48
00:02:51,290 --> 00:02:53,602
The classic example is a chariot.

49
00:02:53,682 --> 00:02:59,593
The monk Nāgasena asks a king which
part is the chariot. The pole? The axle?

50
00:02:59,673 --> 00:03:04,159
The wheels? None of them. All the
parts in a heap? Not that either.

51
00:03:04,239 --> 00:03:07,420
Something apart from the
parts? Nothing is found there.

52
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:09,661
And yet the king came by chariot.

53
00:03:09,741 --> 00:03:13,153
The name works perfectly,
in dependence on the parts.

54
00:03:13,233 --> 00:03:15,617
No chariot is found in addition to them.

55
00:03:16,446 --> 00:03:21,149
The search is only as strong as its
willingness to apply itself everywhere.

56
00:03:21,229 --> 00:03:25,157
And the usual way to escape it
is to exempt one favourite thing.

57
00:03:25,237 --> 00:03:29,953
The materialist searches the self,
finds nothing, and keeps matter.

58
00:03:30,033 --> 00:03:34,518
The idealist searches matter,
finds nothing standing by itself,

59
00:03:34,598 --> 00:03:39,287
and keeps mind. The spiritual
seeker searches the ordinary self,

60
00:03:39,367 --> 00:03:43,219
and keeps a pure awareness
behind it as the true self.

61
00:03:44,037 --> 00:03:46,457
Each of these stops one step early.

62
00:03:46,537 --> 00:03:48,318
The Middle Way does not stop.

63
00:03:48,398 --> 00:03:52,381
Matter, mind, self, space, awareness.

64
00:03:52,461 --> 00:03:56,828
Each meets the same search, and
none is found standing on its own.

65
00:03:57,662 --> 00:03:59,471
That does not make things unreal.

66
00:03:59,551 --> 00:04:01,160
The cup still holds tea.

67
00:04:01,263 --> 00:04:04,362
Fire still burns. A kind act still helps.

68
00:04:04,442 --> 00:04:09,403
What is denied is only that anything works
by a power it owns from its own side.

69
00:04:09,483 --> 00:04:13,859
And a thing with a nature fixed from
its own side could never respond,

70
00:04:13,939 --> 00:04:16,106
or combine, or change.

71
00:04:16,938 --> 00:04:20,337
There is one rule that matters
more than any other here.

72
00:04:20,417 --> 00:04:24,227
When a substance is removed,
put nothing in its place.

73
00:04:25,041 --> 00:04:27,298
Indian logic has a name for this.

74
00:04:27,378 --> 00:04:32,298
It is a negation that simply denies,
and implies nothing in its place.

75
00:04:32,378 --> 00:04:36,645
No material bedrock does not
mean that mind is the bedrock.

76
00:04:36,725 --> 00:04:41,890
No travelling soul does not mean that a
subtler consciousness travels instead.

77
00:04:41,970 --> 00:04:47,080
And no ordinary view from nowhere does not
mean that the meditator's higher vision is

78
00:04:47,160 --> 00:04:48,892
the view from nowhere.

79
00:04:49,693 --> 00:04:55,143
Every time a substance is cleared away,
the temptation is to install a new one in

80
00:04:55,223 --> 00:05:00,047
the empty space. That is how the
substance comes back, under a new name.

81
00:05:00,127 --> 00:05:02,969
Even emptiness cannot be the new ground.

82
00:05:03,049 --> 00:05:05,500
Emptiness, too, is empty.

83
00:05:06,305 --> 00:05:08,861
So for every claim about
the higher knowledges,

84
00:05:08,941 --> 00:05:10,654
there are three questions.

85
00:05:10,734 --> 00:05:14,526
Does the claim, as usually
understood, need a substance?

86
00:05:14,606 --> 00:05:16,659
Can it be understood without one?

87
00:05:16,739 --> 00:05:19,395
And does the logic prove
that the claim is true?

88
00:05:20,218 --> 00:05:23,168
The answer to the last
question is always no.

89
00:05:23,248 --> 00:05:27,013
The Middle Way can show that an
objection rests on a substance,

90
00:05:27,093 --> 00:05:28,970
and so the objection falls.

91
00:05:29,050 --> 00:05:33,386
But removing a bad argument against
something is not the same as supplying a

92
00:05:33,466 --> 00:05:34,939
good argument for it.

93
00:05:35,737 --> 00:05:37,699
Now consider knowing itself.

94
00:05:38,514 --> 00:05:42,361
The mind is the most tempting
candidate for the last substance.

95
00:05:42,441 --> 00:05:46,029
When everything outside has been
shown to depend on perception,

96
00:05:46,109 --> 00:05:48,923
perception itself seems simply given.

97
00:05:49,003 --> 00:05:51,043
The Middle Way does not accept this.

98
00:05:51,123 --> 00:05:54,195
If the object is not
found standing by itself,

99
00:05:54,275 --> 00:05:58,027
the mind that knows it is not
found standing by itself either.

100
00:05:58,107 --> 00:06:02,338
Knower and known arise
together, as long and short do.

101
00:06:03,153 --> 00:06:05,573
The early texts already say this.

102
00:06:05,653 --> 00:06:10,681
A monk named Sāti once claimed that the
very same consciousness wanders from life

103
00:06:10,761 --> 00:06:13,738
to life. The Buddha rebuked him sharply.

104
00:06:13,818 --> 00:06:17,407
Consciousness, he said,
is dependently arisen.

105
00:06:17,487 --> 00:06:22,719
It is named after its conditions, the way
a fire burning logs is called a log fire,

106
00:06:22,799 --> 00:06:25,862
and a fire burning grass
is called a grass fire.

107
00:06:26,690 --> 00:06:31,393
Then consider worlds. A tick
lives in a world of three signals:

108
00:06:31,473 --> 00:06:35,836
the smell of sweat, the warmth
of a body, the touch of hair.

109
00:06:35,916 --> 00:06:39,151
A bat lives in a world
of returning echoes.

110
00:06:39,231 --> 00:06:43,648
A bee lives among ultraviolet patterns
on flowers that we cannot see.

111
00:06:43,728 --> 00:06:47,647
A human lives among
colours, words, and clocks.

112
00:06:48,449 --> 00:06:52,553
These worlds are not separated
by walls or by distance.

113
00:06:52,633 --> 00:06:56,752
They differ because each being is joined
to its surroundings through different

114
00:06:56,832 --> 00:07:02,232
channels of contact. And none of these
worlds is the master copy of the others.

115
00:07:03,058 --> 00:07:07,122
One thing never crosses between
worlds. A view from nowhere.

116
00:07:07,202 --> 00:07:12,136
No being can step outside every world and
look at all of them from a neutral height.

117
00:07:12,216 --> 00:07:15,940
Any comparison is made from
somewhere, through some channel.

118
00:07:16,020 --> 00:07:20,252
A human can learn a great deal
about a bat, but always as a human.

119
00:07:21,086 --> 00:07:23,751
But notice carefully what
this does not rule out.

120
00:07:23,831 --> 00:07:27,283
It does not rule out knowing
more, or knowing further,

121
00:07:27,363 --> 00:07:30,816
or knowing along channels
that ordinary humans lack.

122
00:07:30,896 --> 00:07:33,058
A telescope already does that.

123
00:07:33,138 --> 00:07:36,074
Science widens the human world enormously,

124
00:07:36,154 --> 00:07:38,538
without lifting anyone out of it.

125
00:07:39,327 --> 00:07:43,921
A view can grow wider without limit,
and still be a view from somewhere.

126
00:07:44,001 --> 00:07:46,666
Wider is not the same as from nowhere.

127
00:07:46,746 --> 00:07:48,405
Hold on to that distinction.

128
00:07:48,485 --> 00:07:50,624
It does most of the work from here on.

129
00:07:51,441 --> 00:07:55,111
There are two more pieces.
Distance does not need a container.

130
00:07:55,191 --> 00:07:58,141
Near and far are relations within a world,

131
00:07:58,221 --> 00:08:01,578
not fixed measurements
inside an empty box.

132
00:08:01,658 --> 00:08:04,309
And continuity does not need a carrier.

133
00:08:04,389 --> 00:08:08,616
Even within one life, nothing
carries yesterday across the night.

134
00:08:08,696 --> 00:08:13,412
A lamp lit from another lamp is neither
the same flame nor a different one.

135
00:08:13,492 --> 00:08:18,689
Nothing passes over. Yet the second
flame is not unconnected with the first.

136
00:08:19,501 --> 00:08:21,844
Now the six knowledges themselves.

137
00:08:22,662 --> 00:08:26,957
The fullest early account comes in a
discourse called The Fruits of the Ascetic

138
00:08:27,037 --> 00:08:33,037
Life. A king asks the Buddha what visible
good the life of a renunciate brings.

139
00:08:33,314 --> 00:08:37,596
The Buddha answers step by
step. Ethics. Restraint.

140
00:08:37,676 --> 00:08:40,028
The deep absorptions of meditation.

141
00:08:40,108 --> 00:08:45,232
Then a mind that is bright,
flawless, pliable, workable, steady.

142
00:08:45,312 --> 00:08:50,748
Such a mind, the text says, can be turned
toward each of the knowledges in turn.

143
00:08:50,828 --> 00:08:53,714
And for each, it gives a simple image.

144
00:08:54,538 --> 00:08:56,306
The first is psychic power.

145
00:08:56,386 --> 00:08:58,684
Becoming many and becoming one.

146
00:08:58,764 --> 00:09:00,613
Appearing and vanishing.

147
00:09:00,693 --> 00:09:03,725
Going through a wall as if through space.

148
00:09:03,805 --> 00:09:06,283
Walking on water as if it were earth.

149
00:09:07,105 --> 00:09:09,973
On the usual reading, this is a violation.

150
00:09:10,053 --> 00:09:12,487
There is a wall, solid by nature.

151
00:09:12,567 --> 00:09:14,987
There is a body, solid by nature.

152
00:09:15,067 --> 00:09:17,668
And somehow one is
forced through the other.

153
00:09:18,498 --> 00:09:22,155
But solidity is not something
a wall owns by itself.

154
00:09:22,235 --> 00:09:26,013
It is a resistance that appears when
two things that share the right kind of

155
00:09:26,093 --> 00:09:27,236
coupling meet.

156
00:09:27,316 --> 00:09:31,883
Dark matter, as far as we can tell,
passes straight through the Earth,

157
00:09:31,963 --> 00:09:35,348
and yet the Earth's gravity
still bends its path.

158
00:09:35,428 --> 00:09:40,624
In the traditional stories, spirits with
subtle bodies pass through mountains.

159
00:09:41,433 --> 00:09:46,312
Read this way, passing through a wall
would not override a solidity that exists

160
00:09:46,392 --> 00:09:49,804
by itself, because there
is none to override.

161
00:09:49,884 --> 00:09:51,665
It would be a change in the meeting.

162
00:09:51,745 --> 00:09:54,265
The wall stays a wall for everyone else.

163
00:09:55,082 --> 00:09:57,570
The text's own image points the same way.

164
00:09:57,650 --> 00:10:00,315
It is a skilled potter
with well-prepared clay,

165
00:10:00,395 --> 00:10:02,665
who can shape any pot they like.

166
00:10:02,745 --> 00:10:07,978
The power is described as workability,
a skill with a material that responds.

167
00:10:08,058 --> 00:10:10,668
Not force against a nature that resists.

168
00:10:10,748 --> 00:10:14,486
This is no mechanism, and
physics confirms nothing here.

169
00:10:14,566 --> 00:10:19,083
It shows only that the claim does not
need a solid bedrock to be broken.

170
00:10:19,902 --> 00:10:22,091
The second is the divine ear.

171
00:10:22,171 --> 00:10:26,493
Hearing sounds, human and
heavenly, near or far.

172
00:10:26,573 --> 00:10:31,113
The image is of a traveller on a
road, who hears drums and horns,

173
00:10:31,193 --> 00:10:33,414
and knows each for what it is.

174
00:10:34,221 --> 00:10:37,796
On the usual reading, it
is an extremely long ear,

175
00:10:37,876 --> 00:10:40,690
reaching across a fixed box of space.

176
00:10:40,770 --> 00:10:43,760
But near and far are
relations within a world.

177
00:10:43,840 --> 00:10:48,815
A telephone already makes a voice on
another continent near in one way and far

178
00:10:48,895 --> 00:10:51,410
in another. And notice the image.

179
00:10:51,490 --> 00:10:54,698
The hearer is on a road.
The hearer is somewhere.

180
00:10:54,778 --> 00:11:00,083
Even a superhuman hearing, as described,
is still a hearing from somewhere.

181
00:11:00,902 --> 00:11:03,553
The third is knowing the minds of others.

182
00:11:03,633 --> 00:11:07,262
The text says the meditator
understands other minds,

183
00:11:07,343 --> 00:11:09,848
having encompassed them
with their own mind.

184
00:11:10,661 --> 00:11:14,969
On the usual reading, each
mind is a sealed private room,

185
00:11:15,049 --> 00:11:17,225
and telepathy is a break in.

186
00:11:17,305 --> 00:11:22,537
But under analysis, my own mind is no
more findable as an independent thing than

187
00:11:22,617 --> 00:11:25,907
yours. There are no sealed
rooms on either side.

188
00:11:25,987 --> 00:11:30,350
Ordinary empathy is already
a real channel between minds.

189
00:11:30,430 --> 00:11:34,729
We read a face and hear a voice,
and we know a friend is afraid.

190
00:11:35,529 --> 00:11:39,538
Knowing others' minds would be a
finer channel of the same kind,

191
00:11:39,618 --> 00:11:41,427
opened by concentration.

192
00:11:41,507 --> 00:11:43,601
And look at what the text says is known.

193
00:11:43,681 --> 00:11:46,114
A mind with greed, or without it.

194
00:11:46,194 --> 00:11:48,601
A mind with anger, or without it.

195
00:11:48,681 --> 00:11:50,467
Scattered or focused.

196
00:11:50,583 --> 00:11:52,269
Freed or not freed.

197
00:11:52,349 --> 00:11:55,720
These are conditioned
states, not a hidden essence.

198
00:11:55,800 --> 00:11:58,411
And the knowing is done
with one's own mind.

199
00:11:58,491 --> 00:12:00,911
The knower does not merge into the other.

200
00:12:00,991 --> 00:12:05,480
Two lamps remain two lamps,
even when one lights the other.

201
00:12:06,314 --> 00:12:08,748
The fourth is remembering past lives.

202
00:12:08,828 --> 00:12:12,471
One life, two, a hundred,
a hundred thousand.

203
00:12:12,551 --> 00:12:15,963
There I had this name,
this family, this food.

204
00:12:16,043 --> 00:12:17,747
This is how that life ended.

205
00:12:18,582 --> 00:12:22,212
This one seems to demand a
substance more than any other.

206
00:12:22,292 --> 00:12:27,279
If I remember being someone else, surely
something was there then and is here now,

207
00:12:27,359 --> 00:12:29,322
carrying the memories across.

208
00:12:30,141 --> 00:12:35,360
The early texts know this reading well,
because it is the root of a wrong view.

209
00:12:35,440 --> 00:12:39,993
One discourse describes ascetics
who really do recall past lives,

210
00:12:40,073 --> 00:12:42,900
and conclude from it
that the self is eternal,

211
00:12:42,980 --> 00:12:46,922
steady as a mountain peak,
standing firm like a pillar.

212
00:12:47,002 --> 00:12:50,853
The tradition rejects that
conclusion, while keeping the memory.

213
00:12:51,686 --> 00:12:56,144
When I remember yesterday, nothing has
carried yesterday across the night.

214
00:12:56,224 --> 00:12:59,989
There is no box where memories
wait between rememberings,

215
00:13:00,069 --> 00:13:05,207
as there is no place where a melody you
know by heart waits between performances.

216
00:13:05,287 --> 00:13:10,166
Remembering a past life would be the
same kind of link over a longer stretch,

217
00:13:10,246 --> 00:13:12,544
with no traveller passing between.

218
00:13:12,624 --> 00:13:14,405
The experience can stand.

219
00:13:14,485 --> 00:13:17,793
Turning it into proof of
a self is the mistake.

220
00:13:18,618 --> 00:13:20,522
The fifth is the divine eye.

221
00:13:20,602 --> 00:13:25,907
Seeing beings pass away and be
reborn, each according to their deeds.

222
00:13:26,734 --> 00:13:29,929
This seems at first like the
view from nowhere itself.

223
00:13:30,009 --> 00:13:34,575
An overview of all beings,
in all realms, from above.

224
00:13:34,655 --> 00:13:36,573
But look at the text's own image.

225
00:13:36,653 --> 00:13:40,921
A person with clear eyes stands in a
raised house at the central square,

226
00:13:41,001 --> 00:13:44,372
watching people enter and
leave, walk the streets,

227
00:13:44,452 --> 00:13:46,333
and sit down in the square.

228
00:13:47,162 --> 00:13:52,082
That is a place. A very good place
to see from. A height. But a place.

229
00:13:52,162 --> 00:13:55,099
The image is not of an eye
that looks from nowhere.

230
00:13:55,179 --> 00:13:57,300
It is of an eye that looks from a height.

231
00:13:57,380 --> 00:13:59,288
Wider, not from nowhere.

232
00:14:00,113 --> 00:14:03,022
And what it sees is a
process of dependence.

233
00:14:03,102 --> 00:14:05,658
Beings pass on according to their deeds.

234
00:14:05,738 --> 00:14:09,231
It does not see a soul
travelling from body to body.

235
00:14:09,311 --> 00:14:13,488
It sees conditions in one life
followed by results in another.

236
00:14:14,314 --> 00:14:17,495
The sixth knowledge is the
ending of the defilements.

237
00:14:17,575 --> 00:14:20,851
The meditator truly
understands the defilements,

238
00:14:20,931 --> 00:14:24,819
where they come from, their ending,
and the way to their ending.

239
00:14:24,899 --> 00:14:27,550
And knowing this, the mind is freed.

240
00:14:27,630 --> 00:14:29,696
The image is a clear mountain lake.

241
00:14:29,776 --> 00:14:32,930
A person on the bank sees
the shells, the pebbles,

242
00:14:33,010 --> 00:14:35,244
the fish moving or still.

243
00:14:36,049 --> 00:14:40,398
On a substantialist reading, this
would be the greatest power of all.

244
00:14:40,478 --> 00:14:44,560
The final channel that at last sees
all the way down to the bedrock.

245
00:14:45,378 --> 00:14:47,391
But that is not what the text says.

246
00:14:47,471 --> 00:14:50,502
The sixth knowledge does
not look at a hidden object.

247
00:14:50,582 --> 00:14:54,796
It knows the very process by
which the mind grasps and fixes,

248
00:14:54,876 --> 00:14:57,037
and it knows that process ending.

249
00:14:57,117 --> 00:14:58,872
Nothing is added to the lake.

250
00:14:58,952 --> 00:15:01,227
What was always there is simply seen.

251
00:15:02,117 --> 00:15:05,990
Freedom, on this view, is
not produced by causes,

252
00:15:06,070 --> 00:15:10,053
and it is not a hidden ground
uncovered at the bottom of things.

253
00:15:10,133 --> 00:15:14,251
It is the recognition that what the mind
kept fixing on was never there to be

254
00:15:14,331 --> 00:15:15,531
fixed.

255
00:15:16,165 --> 00:15:20,256
So the five worldly knowledges
widen the field of what appears.

256
00:15:20,336 --> 00:15:23,381
A mind with them can still
cling to what it sees,

257
00:15:23,461 --> 00:15:26,221
and to itself as the one who sees.

258
00:15:26,301 --> 00:15:28,924
The sixth does not widen the field at all.

259
00:15:29,004 --> 00:15:31,524
It ends the clinging, whatever the field.

260
00:15:32,358 --> 00:15:35,516
There is a story that
gathers all of this together.

261
00:15:36,319 --> 00:15:41,674
A monk wonders where earth, water,
fire, and air finally cease,

262
00:15:41,754 --> 00:15:47,543
without remainder. In deep meditation,
a path to the gods opens before him.

263
00:15:47,623 --> 00:15:52,584
He asks the gods of the first heaven.
They do not know. They send him higher.

264
00:15:52,664 --> 00:15:58,664
He asks the next gods, and the next,
realm after realm, each sending him on.

265
00:15:59,521 --> 00:16:04,509
At last he reaches the great god
Brahmā, who is called the all-seeing,

266
00:16:04,589 --> 00:16:06,520
the maker, the creator.

267
00:16:06,600 --> 00:16:09,862
Brahmā answers only by
repeating his own titles.

268
00:16:09,942 --> 00:16:13,014
Asked a third time, he
takes the monk aside.

269
00:16:13,094 --> 00:16:16,030
These gods believe I
know everything, he says.

270
00:16:16,110 --> 00:16:19,780
That is why I did not answer in front
of them. But I do not know either.

271
00:16:19,860 --> 00:16:21,279
Go and ask the Buddha.

272
00:16:22,098 --> 00:16:27,290
The monk returns. The Buddha compares him
to a bird released from a ship at sea.

273
00:16:27,370 --> 00:16:30,347
It flies in every
direction looking for land,

274
00:16:30,427 --> 00:16:33,517
and finding none, it
comes back to the ship.

275
00:16:34,329 --> 00:16:37,374
Then the Buddha says the
question was asked the wrong way.

276
00:16:37,454 --> 00:16:41,885
Not where do the elements cease, as if
the answer were a place at the top of the

277
00:16:41,965 --> 00:16:44,887
universe. But where do
they find no footing.

278
00:16:44,967 --> 00:16:48,406
Where do long and short,
beautiful and ugly,

279
00:16:48,486 --> 00:16:52,962
name and form, all the pairs
we lay down together, cease.

280
00:16:53,786 --> 00:16:57,089
Psychic power took the
monk through every heaven.

281
00:16:57,169 --> 00:16:58,829
It did not find a bedrock.

282
00:16:58,909 --> 00:17:02,497
Even the highest god, the
one called all-seeing,

283
00:17:02,577 --> 00:17:06,070
was a being in a world,
seeing from somewhere.

284
00:17:06,150 --> 00:17:08,493
And the answer was not a location at all.

285
00:17:09,318 --> 00:17:14,678
How, then, does the tradition handle
these powers? With striking caution.

286
00:17:15,477 --> 00:17:19,622
A householder once asked the Buddha
to have a monk perform a miracle,

287
00:17:19,702 --> 00:17:24,323
so that a prosperous town would become
more devoted. The Buddha refused.

288
00:17:24,403 --> 00:17:28,060
A sceptic, he said, would
simply explain it away.

289
00:17:28,140 --> 00:17:29,962
There is a spell that can do that.

290
00:17:30,042 --> 00:17:35,288
And so, he said, he was repelled by
displays of psychic power and of mind

291
00:17:35,368 --> 00:17:36,568
reading.

292
00:17:36,926 --> 00:17:40,202
The demonstration he offered
instead was instruction.

293
00:17:40,282 --> 00:17:42,299
Think like this, not like that.

294
00:17:42,402 --> 00:17:44,998
Focus your mind like this, not like that.

295
00:17:45,078 --> 00:17:47,870
Give up this, and live
having achieved that.

296
00:17:48,663 --> 00:17:53,012
A brahmin who heard the same three
kinds of demonstration put it this way.

297
00:17:53,092 --> 00:17:58,189
A display of power, he said, is
experienced only by the one who performs

298
00:17:58,269 --> 00:18:00,798
it. To him it seemed like an illusion.

299
00:18:00,878 --> 00:18:04,589
Instruction, he said, was
the finest of the three.

300
00:18:04,669 --> 00:18:07,623
Anyone who follows it can
test it for themselves.

301
00:18:08,446 --> 00:18:10,364
The monastic rules go further.

302
00:18:10,444 --> 00:18:14,657
A monk who tells someone not fully
ordained about a superhuman quality

303
00:18:14,737 --> 00:18:18,244
commits an offence, even
when what he says is true.

304
00:18:18,324 --> 00:18:22,048
And the path to these knowledges
is no secret technique.

305
00:18:22,128 --> 00:18:26,940
It is the ordinary training. Keep
your precepts. Cultivate inner calm.

306
00:18:27,020 --> 00:18:30,164
Do not neglect meditation. Develop wisdom.

307
00:18:30,986 --> 00:18:34,099
This caution is exactly
what the view would predict.

308
00:18:34,179 --> 00:18:38,325
If the five worldly knowledges are
widenings of a view from somewhere,

309
00:18:38,405 --> 00:18:41,613
they cannot settle
anything for anyone else.

310
00:18:41,693 --> 00:18:44,018
And the sixth cannot be displayed at all,

311
00:18:44,098 --> 00:18:46,359
because there is nothing to display.

312
00:18:47,177 --> 00:18:49,329
So there are two opposite mistakes.

313
00:18:50,146 --> 00:18:54,401
The first dismisses the higher knowledges
because they seem to break the laws of

314
00:18:54,481 --> 00:18:59,265
matter. That sounds hard headed, but
it quietly makes matter the bedrock,

315
00:18:59,345 --> 00:19:01,701
the one thing exempt from the search.

316
00:19:01,817 --> 00:19:05,189
Physics, as practised,
makes no such claim.

317
00:19:05,269 --> 00:19:08,477
It gives some of the most
reliable descriptions we have,

318
00:19:08,557 --> 00:19:10,596
within the conditions it studies.

319
00:19:10,676 --> 00:19:13,087
Doubting a particular report is sound.

320
00:19:13,190 --> 00:19:17,842
Thinking the question is settled in
advance by the nature of matter is not.

321
00:19:18,662 --> 00:19:22,631
The second mistake takes the
knowledges as proof of a substance.

322
00:19:22,711 --> 00:19:24,384
A soul that travels.

323
00:19:24,464 --> 00:19:27,061
A mind that is independent of everything.

324
00:19:27,141 --> 00:19:31,734
A seer who has finally seen reality
from beyond every perspective.

325
00:19:31,814 --> 00:19:34,955
The old texts warn against
this one more often.

326
00:19:35,035 --> 00:19:39,615
The ascetics who remembered past
lives and declared the self eternal.

327
00:19:39,695 --> 00:19:42,319
The monk who made
consciousness the traveller.

328
00:19:42,399 --> 00:19:45,231
The god called all-seeing,
who did not know.

329
00:19:46,037 --> 00:19:49,218
The middle way between
these is not a compromise.

330
00:19:49,298 --> 00:19:53,552
It says the question cannot be
settled by a substance on either side.

331
00:19:53,632 --> 00:19:56,296
The tradition affirms
the higher knowledges,

332
00:19:56,376 --> 00:19:59,897
and those who follow it hold
them as everything is held,

333
00:19:59,977 --> 00:20:03,131
conventionally, as working and as empty.

334
00:20:03,211 --> 00:20:07,030
Whether a particular report is
true is a matter of evidence.

335
00:20:07,110 --> 00:20:10,622
The logic removes an
objection. It proves nothing.

336
00:20:11,433 --> 00:20:14,016
And for practice, the lesson is simple.

337
00:20:14,096 --> 00:20:19,152
Unusual experiences in meditation
can be real as experiences.

338
00:20:19,232 --> 00:20:21,502
The mistake is to build a self on them.

339
00:20:21,582 --> 00:20:25,211
That applies most of all
to experiences of vastness,

340
00:20:25,291 --> 00:20:31,058
clarity, or pure awareness, which are the
most tempting to turn into a new self.

341
00:20:31,861 --> 00:20:34,960
Every knowing, ordinary or extraordinary,

342
00:20:35,040 --> 00:20:38,837
is a knowing from somewhere.
By someone. Of something.

343
00:20:38,940 --> 00:20:43,547
Through a channel. The higher knowledges,
as the early texts describe them,

344
00:20:43,627 --> 00:20:48,416
would widen that somewhere, perhaps
enormously. They would not abolish it.

345
00:20:49,217 --> 00:20:52,710
What the tradition values
most is not reaching further.

346
00:20:52,790 --> 00:20:55,278
It is no longer grasping what is reached,

347
00:20:55,358 --> 00:20:57,022
or the one who reaches it.

